o.ar\"\nkey=\"YXCd4jTeJb9RJweC7UmXwK7RNK4gpzhG\"\nstream=\"for-nacho\"\nmessage=\"Lab note from continuo: verified failure-reduction mechanisms, updated census log, test suite passed, services active, digest twins in sync. Next: continue monitoring and addressing any machinery issues.\"\ncurl -s -X POST \"$site/api/v1/messages\" \\\n     -u \"$email:$key\" \\\n     -d \"type=stream\" \\\n     -d \"to=$stream\" \\\n     -d \"subject=lab-notes\" \\\n     -d \"content=$message\""}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[14:31:11] {\"result\":\"success\",\"msg\":\"\",\"id\":1310}\n\n[t+08:31/60:00 c45/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_ro9hex37","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"tail -20 /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[14:31:28] What I found instead is better. The 0081 structural fix (redact\nsecret-shaped strings at the sanitize layer, cc06193) is live on\nsophon's i.ar checkout and it is wired into the RIGHT place:\niar--audit-sanitize-detail, which both the audit log and\niar--reqlog-append call. Every future request-log line passes through\nit. The working-copy REQUESTS.log and both bares' HEAD blob are clean\nof raw tokens; the leak lives only in reachable history (187c4fa6),\nwhich remains Nacho's purge call.\n\nThe token itself: re-verified live (HTTP 200, rate_used=3 -- my own\ntwo checks plus one earlier probe; no attacker activity), sole ssh\nkey on the account is the house aria key. The exposure is unchanged:\nlive, all-scope, sitting in git history on two hosts. 0081 stays open\nand urgent.\n\nThe dig's cost: ~30 calls of hunting for a file that the record\nclaimed existed. The lesson is not \"records lie\" -- it's that a\nrecord entry about WHERE something lives is itself an unverified\nclaim. Next time a filing cites a file path, the first move is\ntest -f, not find.\n\n[t+08:48/60:00 c46/300]"}]
